      "pins": ["J2.4"],
      "nets": ["GND", "PWM"],
      "observed": "J2 pin 4 is connected directly to the GND net, while the PWM net contains J4.1, R2.1, R5.2, U2.6 and U3.6 but not J2.4.",
      "expected": "For a standard 4-pin fan connector, pin 4 is the PWM control input; it should be connected to the PWM control net or left unconnected if the fan is controlled by switching its supply, not tied to ground.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"4\") (name \"GND\") ... (node (ref \"J2\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"Pin_4\") (pintype \"passive\")) ...)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"9\") (name \"PWM\") ... (node (ref \"J4\") (pin \"1\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"6\") ... (node (ref \"U3\") (pin \"6\") ...)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "an ATtiny-driven fan controller"}
      ],
      "impact": "A standard 4-pin fan plugged into J2 will have its PWM pin held low. This can prevent normal PWM speed control and may cause the fan to run at minimum speed or fail to start, making the board behave differently from an intended fan controller.",
      "confidence": 0.65
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files show a likely wiring anomaly: the 4-pin FAN connector's pin 4 is tied to GND instead of being connected to the PWM control net or left open, which conflicts with standard 4-pin fan interface practice."
}

